/**
@page building_and_usage Building and using libopenwch

@tableofcontents

@section build_library Building the library

From the repository root:

@code
make                       # both families
make TARGETS=ch32v0        # one family
make clean
@endcode

The build produces:

@code
lib/libopenwch_ch32v0.a
lib/libopenwch_ch5xx58x.a
lib/libopenwch_mini_libc_ch32v0.a
lib/libopenwch_mini_libc_ch5xx58x.a
@endcode

Generated interrupt files are written under
<code>include/libopenwch/&lt;family&gt;/nvic.h</code> and
<code>lib/&lt;family&gt;/vector_*.c</code>.  They are untracked build artifacts;
edit <code>irq.json</code> or the generator, never the generated files.
<code>make irq</code> regenerates them without a RISC-V toolchain.

@section build_application An application Makefile

The library is used through the same small set of make modules as libopencm3:

@code
OPENWCH_DIR ?= ../libopenwch
DEVICE      ?= ch32v003f4p6

include $(OPENWCH_DIR)/mk/genlink-config.mk
include $(OPENWCH_DIR)/mk/gcc-config.mk
include $(OPENWCH_DIR)/mk/genlink-rules.mk
include $(OPENWCH_DIR)/mk/gcc-rules.mk

all: $(BINARY).elf
@endcode

`DEVICE` selects the memory map, ISA, archive, and linker script from
`ld/devices.data`.  Applications must not add their own `-march` or `-mabi`:
mixing `ilp32` and `ilp32e` objects silently breaks the ABI.

The application contract is documented in `mk/README`:

* `CPPFLAGS`, `LDLIBS`, `LDFLAGS`, and `LIBDEPS` are append-only.
* `ARCH_FLAGS` and `LDSCRIPT` are replaced by genlink.
* `-nostartfiles` is required: the reset path and vector table come from the
  library, not from the toolchain's crt0.

@section build_nostdlib Freestanding applications

The library itself does not need a C library, but an application often calls
`memcpy()` or `memset()`.  If the toolchain has no suitable newlib multilib,
set:

@code
LIBOPENWCH_NOSTDLIB=1 make
@endcode

This links the per-family mini-libc archive and ignores the toolchain C
library.  Without that switch the toolchain library is used and the mini-libc
archive is not linked, so the two never shadow each other accidentally.

@section build_examples Start from a working project

* <a href="https://github.com/LrkSeraph/libopenwch-template">libopenwch-template</a>
  is the application skeleton: `Makefile`, `src/main.c`, and submodules for
  the library and the flasher.
* <a href="https://github.com/LrkSeraph/libopenwch-examples">libopenwch-examples</a>
  contains seven complete examples grouped by family: CH32V003 blink, UART
  counter, ADC+DMA+UART, and SPI NOR CRC; CH582 blink, UART counter, and BLE
  advertiser.
* <a href="https://github.com/LrkSeraph/libopenwch-tools">libopenwch-tools</a>
  provides `wchlink`, the host-side WCH-LinkE flasher.

@section build_docs Building these documents

`make html` builds the root site and both family API references:

@code
make html
@endcode

Outputs:

| Site | Path |
| --- | --- |
| project root | `doc/html/index.html` |
| CH32V00x API | `doc/ch32v0/html/index.html` |
| CH58x API | `doc/ch5xx58x/html/index.html` |

The build uses Doxygen plus the vendored Doxygen Awesome theme.  It skips
cleanly if Doxygen is not installed; if it is installed in a non-standard
location, set `DOXYGEN=/path/to/doxygen`.
*/
